1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,520
On November the twenty-fifth, the US Army

2
00:00:02,557 --> 00:00:04,600
blew up more than four tonnes of

3
00:00:04,636 --> 00:00:07,665
an explosive that had become too dangerous to move.

4
00:00:07,781 --> 00:00:10,089
It was lead azide, the touchy explosive

5
00:00:10,125 --> 00:00:11,884
used to set off bigger ones.

6
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:14,036
Sixty-eight drums of it had sat in

7
00:00:14,071 --> 00:00:16,067
bunkers in Iowa since at least the

8
00:00:16,102 --> 00:00:19,067
1980s, kept under liquid so it stayed calm.

9
00:00:19,181 --> 00:00:21,326
But this year inspectors found the liquid

10
00:00:21,359 --> 00:00:22,114
had dried up.

11
00:00:22,266 --> 00:00:24,601
The powder had regrown as crystals that

12
00:00:24,637 --> 00:00:26,726
can go off from friction, or just

13
00:00:26,762 --> 00:00:27,777
from being moved.

14
00:00:27,893 --> 00:00:30,697
A plan to dissolve it with chemicals was cancelled.

15
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:32,818
So the Army ringed the drums with

16
00:00:32,852 --> 00:00:35,206
three hundred sand barriers, tonnes of lime

17
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:36,235
and water misters.

18
00:00:36,349 --> 00:00:38,040
At nine in the morning it went

19
00:00:38,071 --> 00:00:39,070
off in two blasts.

20
00:00:39,221 --> 00:00:41,505
A mile away, windows rattled, and a

21
00:00:41,543 --> 00:00:43,065
grey plume drifted south.

22
00:00:43,184 --> 00:00:44,377
Nobody was hurt.

23
00:00:44,541 --> 00:00:46,840
Now EPA teams are testing the soil

24
00:00:46,875 --> 00:00:49,080
and water downwind, to find out where

25
00:00:49,115 --> 00:00:49,988
the lead went.
